(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-13"] = {"13.1":{"s":"Amines are derived from ammonia by replacing one, two or three hydrogens with alkyl or aryl groups. They are primary (1°), secondary (2°) or tertiary (3°) according to the number of groups on the nitrogen. If the groups are all the same the amine is simple, otherwise mixed. Amines are also aliphatic or aromatic, depending on the groups attached to nitrogen.","w":["Amines are classified by the number of groups on the nitrogen. Isopropylamine is a 1° amine, but isopropyl alcohol is a 2° alcohol."]},"13.2":{"s":"Common names give the alkyl groups followed by 'amine': ethylamine, dimethylamine, trimethylamine. In IUPAC names the final -e of the alkane is replaced by -amine: methanamine, propan-2-amine. Groups on nitrogen are shown with the letter N: N-methylmethanamine for a secondary and N,N-dimethylmethanamine for a tertiary amine. Aniline is benzenamine.","w":["Substituents on the nitrogen get the locant N, not a number."]},"13.3":{"s":"Primary amines come from reducing nitro compounds (Sn/HCl, H₂/Ni, LiAlH₄), nitriles (one more carbon than the halide) and amides (same carbons). Gabriel phthalimide synthesis gives only primary aliphatic amines. Hofmann bromamide degradation of an amide gives a primary amine with one carbon less. Alkyl halides with ammonia give a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines and a salt.","w":["Gabriel synthesis gives pure primary aliphatic amines only; it does not work for aryl halides.","Halide to nitrile to amine adds a carbon (step up); Hofmann degradation removes one (step down)."]},"13.4":{"s":"Lower amines are gases with a fishy smell and dissolve in water by hydrogen bonding; solubility falls as the carbon part grows. Primary and secondary amines form hydrogen bonds with each other, so they boil higher than alkanes of the same mass but lower than alcohols. Tertiary amines have no N–H bond, so they cannot hydrogen bond with each other and boil lower.","w":["Boiling point order for isomeric amines: primary > secondary > tertiary."]},"13.5":{"s":"An amine is basic because the lone pair on nitrogen can accept a proton. Alkyl groups push electrons towards nitrogen and stabilise the ammonium ion, so aliphatic amines are stronger bases than ammonia; in water the secondary amines are the strongest. Aryl amines are weaker bases than ammonia, since the lone pair is delocalised into the benzene ring. Basic strength is compared through pKb: a smaller pKb is a stronger base.","w":["In water the order for ethyl and methyl amines is not simply 3° > 2° > 1°: secondary amines are the strongest bases.","Aniline (pKb 9.38) is a much weaker base than ammonia (pKb 4.75)."]},"13.6":{"s":"Amines with excess alkyl halide are alkylated step by step to a quaternary ammonium salt (exhaustive alkylation). Heating the hydroxide of such a salt gives an alkene by Hofmann elimination, which favours the least substituted alkene. Primary amines are acylated by acid chlorides and anhydrides, and only primary amines give the carbylamine (isocyanide) test. With nitrous acid, primary aliphatic and aromatic amines behave differently.","w":["Carbylamine test (foul-smelling isocyanide with CHCl₃ and KOH) is given by primary amines only.","Hofmann elimination gives the least substituted alkene, the opposite of Saytzeff's rule."]},"13.7":{"s":"Arene diazonium salts react in two ways. In displacement reactions the diazo group leaves as nitrogen and is replaced by Cl, Br, CN (Sandmeyer, with Cu salts), I (with KI), F (via the tetrafluoroborate), H or OH. In reactions that keep the diazo group, the salt couples with a phenol or an aromatic amine to give a coloured azo dye. Coupling with β-naphthol in alkali is a test for primary aromatic amines.","w":["Diazonium salts are made cold (273–278 K) because they decompose on warming."]},"13.8":{"s":"Benzenesulfonyl chloride is Hinsberg's reagent, used to tell the three kinds of amine apart. A primary amine gives a sulfonamide that still has an N–H hydrogen, so it dissolves in alkali. A secondary amine gives a sulfonamide with no such hydrogen, which does not dissolve in alkali. A tertiary amine does not react.","w":["Hinsberg's test: primary gives an alkali-soluble product, secondary an alkali-insoluble product, tertiary no reaction."]},"13.9":{"s":"The amino group is a strong activating group and directs new groups to the ortho and para positions, so aromatic amines undergo electrophilic substitution very easily. Aniline with bromine water gives a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline at room temperature. In strongly acidic mixtures, such as nitrating acid, the amino group is protonated and then directs differently, so the product can be different.","w":["Aniline with bromine water gives 2,4,6-tribromoaniline at once; the reaction does not stop at one bromine."]}};
